WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.710
Film Courage: How does a character arrive
for screenwriters?

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.770
Is it something that is very vivid in their
mind like this is almost a real person even

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:07.839
though it is not?

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:10.900
Or is it a hazy, vague thing that develops
over many drafts?

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:11.900
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.190
Media Arts: I think ultimately it’s going
to depend on the writer and I have know writers

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:23.119
that have a great character and they just
want to see what kind of circumstance would

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:25.640
be right for this character to explore.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:30.980
In fact Frank Daniel [Paul’s former Columbia
mentor] used to say that you could see a story

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.719
as a scientific experiment where you take
a known substance and an environment that

00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:42.840
you know and you put them together and you
see how they react, found out what happens.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.620
Some people will say this great character
what kind of circumstance would challenge

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:52.019
them the most and make them explode basically.

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:57.069
But most people tend to come up with a story
I find first and they explore what kind of

00:00:57.069 --> 00:01:03.559
character can that be and would work for my
story that I have.

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:11.640
And in terms of how different writers work,
I know some writers will base it on a person

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:19.009
they know and sometimes that is very useful
and the advantage of that even though the

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:23.229
person they know has nothing to do with that
kind of story is that they can get real specific.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:29.260
Because you know that character, you know
how they are, they are capable of some things,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.359
they are not capable of other things.

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:40.619
But I’ve been in a situation where we based
a person on a script that was in the story

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:41.619
and at the end of the story.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:44.619
And at the end of the draft I was like “That’s
not the right person for that story.”

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:48.479
So I wound up going through I called it a
major character-ectomy.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:49.479
I changed the…

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:50.479
Film Courage: Ouch!

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:51.479
I bet that hurt.

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:57.899
Paul: Right, well actually it worked out in
the end.

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:00.640
So different writers have different processes.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.810
But the key for me is to make it as specific
as you can.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:11.050
I do have student who will say “He’s 19
and a typical Freshman.”

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.800
And it’s like, there is no typical Freshman.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.030
There is no typical anything.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:19.730
Everybody is a little…everybody is unique
and you look for that.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:24.050
But so yes, that’s been my experience.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.730
Film Courage: What about the science of connecting
to character?

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:35.370
Paul: That is something that we again dedicate
a chapter to [in Paul and Dr. Connie Shears

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.120
book entitled The Science of Screenwriting:
The Neuroscience Behind Storytelling Strategies].

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:45.530
One term that gets used that I think can limit
imagination is the idea of a hero.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:51.480
We’ve talked about the Hero’s Journey
and then people say well if I’ve got a hero,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.140
the connotation of is someone who is heroic.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.840
Someone who is likable and does brave things.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.600
That’s not necessarily what is meant by
the Hero’s Journey but that’s the connotation

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.990
that people have.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:11.811
And the fact is that people tend to like to
watch people with flaws and you don’t want

00:03:11.811 --> 00:03:15.270
to eliminate that.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.150
You have people that aren’t necessarily
admirable.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:27.960
In fact one theory we talk about, a psychologist
by the name of Jonathan Gottschall has a book

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:38.970
on this is that we watch characters that are
flawed because we learn from them.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:47.340
That again (and I can get you the exact psychologist
who said it) he said “Stories are flight

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.160
simulators for life.”

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.430
A flight simulator so you can make mistakes
and not crash.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.710
And a story is watching other people make
mistakes so you can learn to not do those

00:03:58.710 --> 00:03:59.980
things.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:04.340
And so the question of does you character
have to be likable?

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:11.320
Well the answer is it’s all about the primacy
effect (first impressions).

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:18.900
In the example of J.P. McMurphy in ONE FLEW
OVER THE CUCKOO’S NEST.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:25.120
How likable is someone who has committed and
who gets into fights all the time and is in

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.650
jail and slacks off and doesn’t want to
work, just wants to play cards.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.510
You tell someone “You know I’ve got this
character..”

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:36.200
I don’t want to spend time with that character,
that’s who he is.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:42.320
But when you first meet him he’s not doing
those things.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.340
When you first meet him he’s being released
and he’s whooping it up for joy and then

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.940
he is out there hanging out with people and
trying to encourage people and looking at

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:51.970
cards and things.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:57.620
So our first impression of him is that and
that allows us to bond with him and then in

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.480
the course of the story we get to see his
flaws come out and how they play into the

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.290
circumstance of that movie.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.710
That movie En Brouge is the same thing.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:20.320
You have a hired gun and now he’s wondering
what to do but you don’t know that when

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.570
you first meet him he’s like a little boy
complaining that he’s stuck in this place

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:29.620
looking at museums and there is a dark side
to it but we get to know him that way and

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:35.760
then we gradually learn about him and by then
we’re on his side.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:43.090
But the question of connecting to a main character
is what exactly happens (we talk about it

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:44.200
in the book).

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.220
There are different theories.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.970
There is this idea that you like a character
so you sympathize with them.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.310
But there is also a theory that you become
the character.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:06:00.640
That you literally go through a process that
one theorist argues is the basis for morality.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.390
Where you go through a process where you can
feel the pain that the other person feels.

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:11.600
Like the example given is the other person
decides that she is going to hurt the man

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:18.480
near her and then imagines what it would be
like to be hurt and imagines that they are

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.360
the ones being hurt and feels the pain and
says “No!

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:23.540
Not going to do it.”

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:27.610
So it’s more than just liking a character.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:34.530
It’s actually a process by which you merge
and become emotionally connected.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:39.230
I don’t know if we talked about this in
the book but there’s this notion of mirror

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.300
neurons, too (in the brain).

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:48.520
That 
looking at someone eating a chocolate pie

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:54.949
fires the same pleasure neurons in your brain
as you eating it so that you…it’s not

00:06:54.949 --> 00:07:01.840
subtle science as of yet but there are strong
connections that audiences make with the character

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:10.669
that allows them to go on this journey and
purge themselves of emotion.

00:07:10.669 --> 00:07:19.410
As Aristotle said many years ago and I think
this poses a challenge (oddly enough) for

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:25.639
virtual reality a few years ago they were
really struggling with a way to make it a

00:07:25.639 --> 00:07:30.570
mass consumption hit like you have JAWS or
STAR WARS.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:36.240
Or at the movie theater you will have a similar
thing going on with public theater where they

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:44.420
are doing something and the problem with that
is that when you surrender to a movie character,

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:45.420
you are surrendering.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.900
You’re not there anymore, you stop existing
and you don’t have agency.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.801
You don’t do anything, you are just watching
it, you’re just enthralled with it and you

00:07:52.801 --> 00:07:53.919
are it.

00:07:53.919 --> 00:07:57.320
But if you’re in virtual reality you still
have agency.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.669
You are not the person there, you are still
making decisions.

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:09.120
So you are inevitably pulled out of it and
great for a game (I’m glad they haven’t

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.840
developed them too far because I would never
do anything but play virtual reality games).

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.600
It’s wonderful but this process of audience
connection with character whether it’s with

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:31.320
movies or storyteller involves real close
connection and therefore learning and you

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:37.059
see a character go through what we call character
arc when they become consciously aware of

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:40.769
something they didn’t know before that’s
a lesson they have to learn.

00:08:40.769 --> 00:08:48.869
Or you have a tragedy where the character
never learns but we learn that they did something

00:08:48.869 --> 00:08:53.170
that they couldn’t recover from (they didn’t
learn in time).

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:58.319
Or you get something really twisted like [ONE
FLEW OVER THE] CUCKOO’S NEXT and we don’t

00:08:58.319 --> 00:08:59.660
want him to learn.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:08.410
It’s a tragedy that happens but something
else happens so there is variations that.

